
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Current-user store it is. I'm assuming "across the app" means the browser side ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m + [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and future forms),
  not the Node [38;5;153msrc/[39m half — those two share no code today. Say if that's wrong.

  Next question: where it persists, which sets its lifetime. This one is genuinely hard to walk back — it's a stored
  format other code and other tabs will come to depend on, and changing it later strands whatever's already on users'
  machines.

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — one tab, cleared when that tab closes. Buys the narrowest exposure and a free "logout on close";
    costs you a fresh login per tab, and a second tab doesn't know about the first.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives reloads, tab closes, and browser restarts, shared across tabs on the origin. Buys real
    persistence and cross-tab consistency; costs you an identity that outlives the browser session, so logout and expiry
    become things you must implement rather than get for free.
  - [1mIn-memory module only[0m — a variable other code reads. Buys simplicity and zero exposure; costs persistence entirely,
    so a reload loses it. This fails your "it should persist" requirement — listing it so the tradeoff is on the table,
    not recommending it.

  One constraint that holds across all three: anything we store here is readable by any script on the page, so it holds
  the identifier only — no password, no token. The password at [38;5;153mapp.js:20[39m never reaches this store.

  [1mI recommend [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m with an explicit expiry stamp[0m — it's what "should persist" asks for, and the expiry keeps the
  identity from living forever, which is the main cost of choosing it.
